---
registryId: arangodb
---

{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is ArangoDB?

**ArangoDB is a native multi-model database that stores key-addressed records,
JSON documents, and graph relationships in one database engine.** Its query language,
AQL, can filter document attributes, follow graph edges, aggregate results, and combine
those operations in one query.

In plain language, one customer record can be retrieved by its key, queried as a
document, and connected to accounts or devices as a graph node. The application does
not need to copy that same fact into three separate databases just to use three access
patterns.

The core invariant is **the stored model must make the application's dominant query
and correctness boundary explicit**. A unified engine does not remove index design,
edge lifecycle, transaction scope, shard placement, replication, or recovery work.

{% callout variant="info" %}
### Native multi-model is a query choice, not three storage engines

ArangoDB's key-value model is a subset of its document model. Graph nodes and edges are
also documents; edge documents add `_from` and `_to` references. The important design
decision is which access path owns each request.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## See one engine through three access paths

Each model makes a different application question direct.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Key-value access" eyebrow="Known identity" icon="key" tone="blue" %}
Every document has an immutable string `_key` inside its collection. The built-in
primary index supports exact `_key` and `_id` lookups without a user-created index.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Document query" eyebrow="Attributes and aggregates" icon="braces" tone="violet" %}
A document is a JSON object whose attributes can be filtered, sorted, projected, and
aggregated with AQL. Secondary indexes should follow real query predicates.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Graph traversal" eyebrow="Paths and neighborhoods" icon="network" tone="green" %}
Vertices live in document collections and directed edges live in edge collections.
AQL can traverse inbound, outbound, or either direction over bounded depths.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

Use the narrowest model that answers the question. A profile lookup does not need a
graph traversal; a fraud neighborhood cannot be discovered from a known-key lookup
alone.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## Choose the model from the query shape

Select an application query, then try each access path. The lab changes the required
index, execution steps, and maintenance cost. It is a decision model based on query
semantics, not a latency or throughput benchmark.

{% interactive-block id="technology/arangodb-performance" dataFile="/api/content/technology/arangodb/data/modeling-query-shapes.json" /%}

Write the expected query before creating collections. Include the known identifiers,
attribute predicates, relationship direction and depth, sort order, projection, result
limit, and the facts that must be transactionally correct.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## Express each access path in AQL

AQL is declarative: describe the documents and paths you need, then inspect the query
plan to verify which indexes, traversals, remote operations, and gathers it uses.

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Known key" %}
Filter by `_key` equality when the collection is known. The built-in primary index is
the intended access path.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/arangodb/code/aql-key-value-lookup.txt" language="text" title="Fetch one document through the primary index" /%}
{% /tab %}
{% tab title="Document filter" %}
Use explicit predicates, sorting, projection, and limits. Create a persistent index
only after the full query shape and write cost are understood.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/arangodb/code/aql-document-query.txt" language="text" title="Filter and project documents" /%}
{% /tab %}
{% tab title="Graph traversal" %}
Bound the direction and depth. Return only the vertex, edge, or path fields the caller
needs, and use `PRUNE` when a condition can stop expansion early.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/arangodb/code/aql-graph-traversal.txt" language="text" title="Traverse exactly two outbound hops" /%}
{% /tab %}
{% tab title="Mixed model" %}
One AQL query can filter starting documents, traverse edge documents, inspect connected
vertices, and return the path as evidence.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/arangodb/code/aql-multi-model-query.txt" language="text" title="Combine document filters and graph evidence" /%}
{% /tab %}
{% /tabs %}

Run `EXPLAIN` before production and `PROFILE` with representative data. Concise AQL
syntax does not prove that a distributed query is selective or local.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## Keep transaction claims inside the deployment boundary

ArangoDB supports ACID transactions, but the exact guarantee depends on deployment and
query scope.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Single server" eyebrow="Broad local boundary" icon="shield" tone="green" %}
Multi-document and multi-collection transactions can be fully ACID when the work stays
inside one server and avoids documented intermediate-commit limits.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Cluster, one document" eyebrow="Native atomic unit" icon="braces" tone="blue" %}
A single-document operation in a cluster is fully ACID. A multi-document query can also
be ACID when its collection has one shard.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Cluster, many shards" eyebrow="Distributed limit" icon="network" tone="amber" %}
Do not claim one atomic transaction across arbitrary shards or collections. Use
OneShard for eligible multi-collection ACID work, or design an application workflow
with idempotency and reconciliation.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

Also separate transaction atomicity from disk and replica acknowledgement. Options such
as `waitForSync`, `replicationFactor`, and `writeConcern` describe different durability
boundaries and should be reviewed explicitly.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## Design shard keys from placement and lookup behavior

In a cluster, every collection is divided into a configured number of shards.
Coordinators route work to DB-Servers, and the default shard key is `_key`.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="List equality lookups" label="Routing" %}
Record which requests know `_key`, tenant, account, region, or another stable ownership
attribute. A Coordinator can target one shard only when it has the shard-key values.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Measure key distribution" label="Balance" %}
Check cardinality, frequency, and the largest value. Every document with the same shard
key value maps together, so a dominant tenant or region can create a hot shard.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Trace cross-collection work" label="Locality" %}
Identify joins, transactions, and graph traversals that cross collection boundaries.
Independent sharding can turn one logical query into network fan-out.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Lock the creation contract" label="Lifecycle" %}
Choose shard keys and shard count before loading data, then test rebalancing, failure,
backup, and restore. Shard-key attributes cannot be changed in place.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

{% callout variant="warn" %}
### A custom shard key changes point-lookup routing

If a collection is sharded by something other than `_key`, a lookup that supplies only
the document key may need to contact every shard. Include the custom shard-key values
when the operation supports a lookup hint, and verify the AQL plan.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## Trace replication and failure at one shard

The topology below models one shard with `replicationFactor: 3` and
`writeConcern: 2`. Switch scenarios to see how follower loss, leader promotion, write
concern, and independent backup change the result. Select any node to inspect its
responsibility.

{% topology-lab dataFile="/api/content/technology/arangodb/data/shard-replication-failure-topology.json" /%}

The model intentionally omits timing. Detection, promotion, resynchronization, and
replacement duration depend on the deployed version, configuration, data size, and
infrastructure. Measure those values in failure drills.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## Make collection assumptions executable

This `arangosh` example creates an illustrative collection with six shards, a tenant
shard key, three configured copies, and a two-copy write concern.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/arangodb/code/arangodb-cluster-collection.js" language="javascript" title="Create a sharded, replicated collection" /%}

These values are not defaults or universal recommendations. Before using them:

1. replay the real tenant distribution and measure the hottest shard;
2. confirm common lookups include `tenantId` when targeted routing matters;
3. verify the cluster has enough DB-Servers for the replication factor;
4. inject follower and leader failures while observing write acknowledgement;
5. calculate physical storage from logical bytes, replicas, indexes, compaction, and
   operational headroom.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## Operate query, shard, and recovery contracts together

Monitor facts that identify which boundary is failing:

- **queries:** execution plans, scanned documents, result rows, remote operations,
  Coordinator memory, traversal depth, branching, and cancellation;
- **indexes:** selectivity, write maintenance, cache behavior, build status, and indexes
  that no observed query uses;
- **shards:** document and byte distribution, request rate per shard, leader placement,
  under-replication, resynchronization, and rebalance progress;
- **writes:** accepted, rejected, timed out, retried, and reconciled operations, plus the
  active `writeConcern` and `waitForSync` contract;
- **recovery:** independent backup age, restore duration, recovered point in time,
  document validation, graph-edge integrity, and application-level verification.

Replication is not a backup. A valid delete or corrupt application write can be copied
to every follower, so recovery needs an independent artifact and a tested restore path.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## Know when ArangoDB is a strong fit

{% concept-grid columns="2" %}
{% concept-card title="Strong fit" eyebrow="Several connected access patterns" icon="check" tone="green" %}
Choose ArangoDB when the same owned facts need document filters, known-key access, and
relationship traversal, and one team can operate their shared transaction, sharding,
backup, and access-control boundary.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Weak fit" eyebrow="One simpler dominant contract" icon="warning" tone="rose" %}
Prefer a simpler store when the workload is only key-value lookup, requires unrestricted
cross-shard ACID transactions, centers on large analytical scans, or needs a graph copy
that is clearly derived from another authoritative database.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

Multi-model capability is valuable when it removes a real synchronization boundary.
It is not a reason to put unrelated workloads into one cluster.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card %}
## Verify decisions against official documentation

These primary Arango references define the semantics used in this lesson:

- [data models: key-value, document, and graph](https://docs.arango.ai/arangodb/3.12/concepts/data-models/);
- [AQL data queries and document access](https://docs.arango.ai/arangodb/stable/aql/data-queries/);
- [primary and edge index behavior](https://docs.arango.ai/arangodb/stable/indexes-and-search/indexing/basics/);
- [cluster sharding and shard-key routing](https://docs.arango.ai/arangodb/3.12/deploy/architecture/data-sharding/);
- [cluster roles, synchronous replication, and automatic failover](https://docs.arango.ai/arangodb/3.12/deploy/cluster/);
- [transaction guarantees and cluster limitations](https://docs.arango.ai/arangodb/stable/develop/transactions/).

Pin the deployed ArangoDB version in runbooks. Defaults, limitations, and operational
procedures can change between releases.
{% /section-card %}

